i think we are now seeing the final days  of this  tragic and terrible episode going on at  the as of style uh steelworks um if you  think back um a week or so ago maybe 10  days ago uh beauty announced that um i  think you just reported again that um he  would seal it off so that not even a fly  could get in and out well that was an  indication that they were finding the  russians were finding it very difficult  to finally subdue the garrison there um  they've now come to the conclusion that  they've got to have some form of victory  to celebrate on monday and it would  appear that they've now resumed a direct  attack uh on the steel works in order to  try and snuff out the remaining part of  the resistance there so that they can  claim on monday that they have captured  mario paul and therefore they have  completed their land corridor from  crimea through the donbass into russia  proper i mean this is a tragedy that's  unfolding in front of our eyes some of  the civilians may get out over two or  three days ceasefire which is being  talked about but for the soldiers in  there i'm afraid the prospect is very  grim and i think we may also see the  very ugly sight of ukrainian prisoners  being paraded through moscow next monday  at their victory parade it happened with  the germans at the end of the second  world war i'm an awful feeling it's  going to happen again on monday  tell us about the significance of this  victory parade  well it is the traditional annual  victory parade marking the end of what  the russians would call the great  patriotic war the second world war when  a roundabout an unquantified figure of  around 20 million russians lost their  lives so they have every right to  celebrate that but of course  they don't have a right to have begun  this war they've got no right to  celebrate this war and therefore i think  we're going to be subjected to some  pretty ugly and awful pictures from  moscow on on monday um as we've said so  many times this attack on ukraine is  totally unnecessary it's disgraceful  it's horrendous and once again just well  done to the ukrainians led by president  zielenski for fighting back so hard  i wonder if you could talk to me  about the uh theater  in mariopol uh we knew last month that  fact in march uh that there had been a  direct hit on the theater where up to a  thousand women and children were  sheltering in the basement of the  theatre we're told that  children had been written in white  letters that could clearly be seen from  the sky in front of the building and yet  it took a direct hit the russians first  of all said it was the ukrainians that  had done it um it transpires  that something like 300 people  originally we were told had been killed  we're now being told that number could  have doubled is that a war crime  it most certainly is a war crime but it  and it's also  ugly characteristic of the way the  russians are conducting uh this war this  operation the special military  operations they're calling it um they  have learned early on in the war that if  they engage the ukrainians chin to chin  toe to toe the russians come off second  best so they've adopted this appalling  tactic of standing off and using  artillery and cruise missiles and many  of those in a very unguided fashion and  i think this is exactly what i did i  think i'm absolutely sure this is what  happened to the theater in mario poll um  an un an unguided weapon well let's be  charitable and say it was an ungodly  weapon because it was deliberate it  makes it even worse but it doesn't  really matter because the outcome of  probably 600 people losing their lives  is quite appalling and undoubtedly by  anybody's estimation this is a war crime  amongst a litany and chapter of other  war crimes the dossier which is being  prepared quite properly to be used at  some time in the future this attack on  the theater was really quite despicable  belarus um saying it's conducting  military drills um as a military man  what's your reading of that  well i don't know really i think um  the relationship between a lot of  belarusians and russia proper is um  somewhat ambivalent  um we've got good evidence that earlier  in the campaign if you remember the big  strike that came down from belarus  to achieve regime change in kiev was in  many ways um  interrupted uh and sabotaged by the  bella russian railway workers who didn't  agree with what the russians were doing  and the russians have based their  logistic plan uh on using the railways  down from belarus but found that they  couldn't hence they used road transport  hence we got this 30-40 kilometer a  tailback which became a huge target  array for for the ukrainians so i think  um president luchashenko's hold on power  in minsk is actually quite tenuous uh  and  those reports i think are propagandist  type reports i don't for a moment think  that russian forces will start to be  ordered by their president down into  ukraine i think this is um this is uh  this is a smoke screen and um typical  russian  anti-propaganda  so anything more that britain can do at  this time we know that uh the ukrainians  want boots on the ground that's not um  going to happen at least for now because  of the consequences if we tiered but  they also want more and more heavy  artillery  yes and their request is absolutely  right um as i suggested earlier uh the  russians have come to the conclusion  that if they take the close fight to the  ukrainians the russians will come off  second best and take huge casualties and  they can't afford more casualties  it's already come out in your program  this morning that over the ten years of  afghanistan the russians lost a lot of  soldiers but they've lost more than that  in the last three months so they are  resorting to artillery which means that  the ukrainians must do the same so  britain the west uh must supply and  indeed are supplying a larger quantities  of artillery pieces and ammunition to go  with it and particularly target  acquisition radars and drones so that  ukrainian artillerymen can pin back  russian artillery uh forces to um  we call counter battery fire to  reduce the pressure on their own front  line soldiers so this is very much  becoming an artillery battle and that's  actually really why the progress by the  russians in the donbass has been so slow  they're reluctant to close with the  enemy but they're much more willing to  shell from a distance and that's why the  ukrainians need all the artillery they  can get to make an effective counter  battery a counter-attack  we've spoken many times about how  i was going to say label but that's the  wrong word how we should describe what  is happening in ukraine and we've  touched on whether or not it is genocide  you i know are closely monitoring the  situation are you any closer to feeling  that it is  well the evidence is is mounting up um  the question really comes as to when  i say sufficient when the volume of war  crimes that can be um analyzed and  recorded is such that it's quite clear  that there is a wider malicious intent  from the very top behind it that when  that is proven then that actually makes  the case for for genocide um okay as  we've said before um back in the balkans  we thought in the mid 90s we would never  get the likes of milosevic and karadix  and maladic into a jail and try them on  charges of of genocide well they have  all been charged and gareth  are imprisoned whether we'll ever get  putin there whether he'll actually ever  live long enough that's another question  but other senior russians may well find  themselves facing an international court  or if not that then the court of history  i think will judge very harshly and i  think the case of genocide is looking  increasingly likely to be proven  and just returning finally and to a  topic we touched on during the course of  the interview 9th of may which is next  monday and whether in your opinion uh  president putin may take the opportunity  then to declare victory in the war in  ukraine and if so  then what happens does he pull his  troops out what happens  well  he may well declare victory because  frankly with his control over the state  media he can say what he likes and  tragically a large proportion of the  russian people will believe what he says  but the fact on the ground is that  there is no way that he has achieved a  victory if mario paul full falls which i  think it probably tragically sadly will  do then he can say that he has achieved  a land corridor from crimea through  donbass to russia  but that's as far that's as far as he  can go if he wants to be truthful about  it um  the sad reality  is that at some point  hopefully in the not too distant future  there will be some form of general  ceasefire  and the war will stop where it is and  where that will be  will be with the russians having  achieved this land corridor probably  having taken control of most of donetsk  and luhans province in the donbass  region and that's where the negotiating  will start but then you have to ask the  question  even if the court of world opinion says  the russians were wrong are they going  to leave  well i don't think they will they won't  leave of their own free accord and no  one's going to throw them out  nato and the west is not going to mount  a an iraq-style scale operation  to throw out the russians from ukraine  in the same way that saddam hussein was  thrown out of of kuwait that just isn't  going to happen so there may have to be  the realization of an awful new status  quo where 10 to 15 of ukraine is  occupied by russians and there's nothing  that can be done about it
 

